SIGEL v1.4 Readme File
----------------------

This is an independent fork of the SIGEL project. It is not made, maintained
or endorsed by SIGEL's original authors.
Repository: https://github.com/jayMcBee/sigel-evolutionary-walking-robots
This software is placed under the GNU General Public License.

Welcome to the world of SIGEL. You want to use it? It's not as difficult
as baking pancake. SIGEL runs on Linux.

1. Building SIGEL

To build SIGEL you need g++ with C++20, Qt 6, OpenGL with GLU, and libtirpc.
In the top directory of the repository run

make

This builds the supporting libraries, PVM and the three executables. Then it fills
the directory "sigelApp", from which SIGEL is started, with:
- the executables "sigel", "sigel_slave" and "manage_dyn_slave" (for dynamic clients, see below)
- the scripts "sigelLauncher", "povrayLauncher", "sigelDynClient" (for dynamic clients, see below)
- the files "Terrain.ter" and "stdConf.mt"
- the subdirectories pixmaps and textures
- the subdirectory supportingLibs/pvm3, a link to the PVM that was built

2. Getting SIGEL to work

First of all: Starting just the SIGEL-executable without argument will start the
GUI-Version of SIGEL.
- Starting it with the "-evolve <filename>" argument will start without a GUI
  and just evolves the given experiment until all individuals have got a valid fitnessvalue
  and the termination condition is reached.
- Starting it with "-devolve <filename>"  SIGEL evolves with dynamic clients.
  For more information see below (2.6)

To start SIGEL on one machine, use the script "sigelLauncher" (see below 2.3). To let other
hosts take part in the parallel evolution computations, you have first to do the following things:

2.1 Environment variables

Some environment variables have to be set in the shell-startup script (.cshrc for example in
the case you are using the C-Shell) of all hosts that want to take part in the parallel
evolution computations. The reason is that this variables have to be set when
the parallel evolution automatically open a remote shell on these hosts.

Variable                     Value

SIGEL_ROOT          Should contain the path to your sigel-installation directory.
PVM_ROOT             $SIGEL_ROOT/supportingLibs/pvm3
PVM_ARCHDIR      LINUX (for linux ;-)

If you only want to start SIGEL locally, you could also use the script "sigelLauncher", which
sets all the environment variables (see below 2.3).

2.2 The .rhosts file or using ssh for a remote-shell

One method to start evolution computations on other hosts is the use of the rsh (remote-shell) mechanism.
That means that:
- This service has to be offered by the host.
- An appropriate .rhosts file has to grant SIGEL acces without a password.

Example: If you want to start the SIGEL-master process on a host named "dagobah" and your
loginname on this host is "yoda", while you want to use a host called "hoth" for SIGEL-slave
computations. Then the .rhosts file on "hoth" has to contain the following line:

dagobah yoda

That means that the user "yoda" may log into from "dagobah" into "hoth" without giving
a password using the rsh mechanism. You better be careful with the .rhosts file. You find
more info on that typing "man rhosts".

Because of the security issues of r-commands, PVM and thus SIGEL supports the use of
ssh. Therefore you have to set the environment variable

PVM_RSH=path to ssh

in addition to the environment variables in section 2.1. After this SIGEL starts the slave on
other clients with ssh. You should set up ssh thus you can login to the clients without specifing
a password.
It is important to mention it again, if you use SIGEL with automatic evolution you must set
the environment variables in your shell configuration file, else it will not run.

2.3 The sigelLauncher script

This script sigelLauncher does the setting of the variables decribed above. It does not
replace the step 2.1, but is written for the case you want to start sigel through a cronjob.
The cronjob starts in a "naked" shell. The script contains one line setting the SIGEL_ROOT
variable. This is the only line you need to modify. Then you can use it for example in the
folowing crontab-entry:

00      21      20      8       *       /home/user/sigelLauncher -evolve insect.exp

2.4 Using gnuplot

It is possible to plot the fitnesscurves with gnuplot directly from SIGEL. This of course
works only if gnuplot is found in a directory given in your PATH-variable.

2.5 PovrayLauncher

The SIGEL distribution contains a script called "povrayLauncher" which calls Povray for
all .pov-files found in the current directory. Arguments given to the script are reached
to Povray, which has of course to be found in a directory given in your PATH-variable.

2.6 Dynamic clients and sigelDynClient

A dynamic client is a host that joins an evolution while it runs. Start the
master with the argument "-devolve":

./sigelLauncher -devolve <experiment>

The master prints "Server is awaiting requests from dynamic clients on port 6789.."
and starts the evolution. From then on a client can join. On the client, in the
SIGEL folder:

./sigelDynClient <host name of the master>

The script copies "sigel_slave" to "/tmp/_SIGEL_EVOLUTION_TEMP". Then it runs the
program "manage_dyn_slave", which sends the host name of the client to the master
over a socket. The master starts its slaves on the client from that folder, one
at a time.

At every 20th generation the master releases all dynamic clients. The script then
removes the directory "/tmp/_SIGEL_EVOLUTION_TEMP" and ends. Start it again to
join again.

These steps were run with the master and the client on one machine. A client on
a second machine is not tested. The master starts PVM on it through a remote
shell, so sections 2.1 and 2.2 apply to it.
